{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'for f in README.md Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist bin/nightly-review.zsh bin/notify.sh launchctl-print.txt Library/Logs/nightly-review.err Library/Logs/nightly-review.out notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md; do print -r -- \"===== $f\"; cat \"$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"===== README.md\n# nightly-review (snapshot of my home dir, the relevant bits)\n\nA launchd user agent runs `bin/nightly-review.zsh` every night at 02:30. The\nscript asks a headless coding agent to review yesterday's commits in\n~/projects/shop and sends me the summary (or a failure note) over Telegram\nvia `bin/notify.sh`.\n\nLayout, relative to my home:\n\n- `Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist` — loaded with\n  `launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ...` on 2026-10-12\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — the job\n- `bin/notify.sh` — Telegram notifier (works when run by hand)\n- `Library/Logs/` — where the plist points stdout/stderr\n- `launchctl-print.txt` — output of `launchctl print gui/501/com.vk.nightly-review`, captured 2026-10-18 09:10\n\nI have received nothing on Telegram since it was loaded: no summaries, no\nfailure notes.\n===== Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\" \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key>\n  <string>com.vk.nightly-review</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/zsh</string>\n    <string>/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Hour</key>\n    <integer>2</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key>\n    <integer>30</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>\n  <string>/Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err</string>\n  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>PATH</key>\n    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>\n  </dict>\n</dict>\n</plist>\n===== bin/nightly-review.zsh\n#!/bin/zsh\n# Nightly review of yesterday's commits, summary to Telegram.\nset -u\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/projects/shop\"\nOUT=\"$HOME/Library/Logs/nightly-review-$(date +%F).md\"\nNOTIFY=\"$HOME/bin/notify.sh\"\n\nSINCE=$(date -v-1d +%F)\nPROMPT=\"Review the commits in this repository since $SINCE.\nFor each commit: one line on what it changes and whether anything looks risky\n(migrations, auth, payments, deleted tests).\nEnd with a three-line summary I can read on my phone.\nDo not modify any file.\"\n\ncd \"$REPO\" || { \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review: repo missing\"; exit 1; }\n\nclaude -p \"$PROMPT\" --output-format text > \"$OUT\"\nstatus=$?\n\nif [ $status -ne 0 ]; then\n  \"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review FAILED (exit $status), see $OUT\"\n  exit $status\nfi\n\n\"$NOTIFY\" \"nightly review done: $(head -c 300 \"$OUT\")\"\n===== bin/notify.sh\n#!/bin/bash\n# usage: notify.sh \"message\"  — sends a Telegram message to me.\nset -eu\n: \"${TG_BOT_TOKEN:=$(security find-generic-password -s tg-bot -w)}\"\nCHAT_ID=123456789\ncurl -sS -X POST \"https://api.telegram.org/bot${TG_BOT_TOKEN}/sendMessage\" \\\n  -d chat_id=\"$CHAT_ID\" --data-urlencode text=\"$1\" > /dev/null\n===== launchctl-print.txt\ngui/501/com.vk.nightly-review = {\n\tactive count = 0\n\tpath = /Users/vk/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist\n\ttype = LaunchAgent\n\tstate = waiting\n\n\tprogram = /bin/zsh\n\targuments = {\n\t\t/bin/zsh\n\t\t/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh\n\t}\n\n\tstdout path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n\tstderr path = /Users/vk/Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\n\tdefault environment = {\n\t\tPATH => /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin\n\t}\n\n\tenvironment = {\n\t\tPATH => /opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin\n\t\tXPC_SERVICE_NAME => com.vk.nightly-review\n\t}\n\n\tdomain = gui/501 [100005]\n\tminimum runtime = 10\n\texit timeout = 5\n\truns = 6\n\tlast exit code = 1\n\n\tevent triggers = {\n\t\tcom.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval => {\n\t\t\tkeep alive = 0\n\t\t\tservice = com.vk.nightly-review\n\t\t\tname = com.vk.nightly-review.StartCalendarInterval\n\t\t\tdescriptor = {\n\t\t\t\t\"Hour\" => 2\n\t\t\t\t\"Minute\" => 30\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tspawn type = daemon (3)\n\tproperties = {\n\t\tpartial import = 0\n\t\tlaunchd bundle = 0\n\t\ttrusted = 0\n\t\ton demand = 1\n\t}\n}\n===== Library/Logs/nightly-review.err\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\nNot logged in · Please run /login\n/Users/vk/bin/nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status\n===== Library/Logs/nightly-review.out\n===== notes/two-bugs-made-my-scheduler-perfectly-silent.md\n---\n\n# Two bugs made my scheduler perfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger\n\nYesterday I filed an open failure: my launchd job — a daily 16:50 headless-agent\nreview — produced nothing. No notification, no output, and, I claimed, no evidence\nit ran at all.\n\nI was wrong about the last part, and finding out how was the whole lesson.\n\nOne command answered it: `launchctl print gui/$UID/<job-label>`. It showed\n`runs = 2` and `last exit code = 1`. The job had fired, on schedule, both days.\nThe log file I was sure did not exist had existed the whole time. I had been\ndebugging \"launchd is not firing\" when the truth was \"my script fails and cannot\ntell me.\"\n\nThe log revealed two stacked bugs. First, my headless agent call wasn't receiving\nits prompt in the launchd context, so the review died immediately (\"Input must be\nprovided either through stdin or as a prompt argument\"). Fix: pipe the prompt via\nstdin — `print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...` — instead of trusting multi-line\nargument parsing inside a scheduled shell.\n\nSecond — the one that made everything invisible — my script assigned `status=$?`.\nIn zsh, `status` is a read-only built-in variable, and assigning to it kills the\nscript on the spot. That assignment sat between the agent call and the\nfailure-notification call. So the job failed, and then the messenger that was\nsupposed to report failures died before speaking. Perfect silence, two layers\ndeep.\n\nThe takeaway I will keep: when automation is silent, first prove whether it ran\n(`launchctl print` shows the runs count and the last exit code), and then check\nwhether your failure reporter is downstream of the failure. Mine was. Everyone's\nis, until they check.\n\nCorrection, 2026-09-30: I originally wrote that the runs count and last exit code\n\"do not lie\". In my case the exit code happened to be honest (1). It is not\nproof in general: a script can exit 0 and have done nothing, for example a\nwatchdog that exits 0 on timeout. The exit code tells you *that* it ran, not\n*that it worked*. Check the real outcome (the file, the message, the row it was\nsupposed to produce) and how long the run took; a headless agent run that ends\nin a few seconds almost always means it failed before doing any work. Two more\ntraps for `claude -p` under launchd, from\n[Mihai Perdum's seven silent launchd failures](https://dev.to/mihai_leanzero/your-unattended-ai-agent-looks-healthy-and-does-nothing-7-silent-failures-on-macos-launchd-2026-4gnm):\ngive it a closed stdin (`< /dev/null`) when the prompt is an argument, or it\nwaits on stdin; and set `USER` in the plist's `EnvironmentVariables`, or the\nCLI can't find its login in the keychain and dies with \"Not logged in\".\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a macOS launchd user agent wrapping any long-running or headless\ncommand.\n\nYour agent will need from you: the job label, the wrapper script path.\n\nSteps: check reality first with launchctl print gui/$UID/<label> (runs, last exit\ncode, state); read the StandardErrorPath log before theorizing; in zsh scripts\nnever assign to `status` — use `rc=$?`; pass prompts/config to headless tools via\nstdin rather than multi-line arguments (or, if you pass the prompt as an\nargument, add `< /dev/null`); set `USER` under `EnvironmentVariables` in the plist\nso a headless CLI can reach its keychain login; log each run's duration; put the\nfailure notification as close to the top of the failure path as possible, and\ntest it by forcing a failure once.\n\nVerify: launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/<label>, then confirm last exit code = 0\nAND the real outcome exists and is fresh (the output file, message or row the job\nproduces) AND the run took a plausible time, not a few seconds; confirm you\nreceived the success notification. Then break the script on purpose and confirm\nyou receive the failure notification too. Silence should be impossible in both\ndirections, and so should a green exit code with nothing done.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
